xt contains \"quest\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"renownProfile":{"knowsBook":true,"publicFame":75,"criticalRenown":68,"controversy":18,"llmAffinity":84,"affinityResidual":-1.53,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.96,"criticalRenown":0.9,"controversy":0.82,"llmAffinity":0.97},"tags":{"craft":"structural-invention","evidence":"synthesis-of-scholarship","stance":"conceptual-strangeness"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"If humans disappeared, most infrastructure would collapse and vanish, while certain artificial materials and cultural artifacts would leave extraordinarily durable traces on the planet.","confidence":0.87},"academicOrientation":{"score":25,"confidence":0.9},"profileConfidence":0.9,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"e88598963f6accc934e8e3dc88aca3e99c050a993fabbcb3c76641edbe9b77a8","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Nature & Environment","secondarySubjects":["Science","Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores the environmental impact of humanity and the potential consequences of our disappearance, positioning it firmly within Nature & Environment, while also touching on scientific and cultural aspects.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"413867a3dde836d707b0c2140acd18f75a1c3e8117f54a79d79e174f45e3eda4","summary":"Journalist Weisman offers an original approach to questions of humanity's impact on the planet. Drawing on the expertise of engineers, atmospheric scientists, art conservators, zoologists, oil refiners, marine biologists, astrophysicists, religious leaders, and paleontologists, he illustrates what the planet might be like today if humans disappeared. He explains how our massive infrastructure would collapse and finally vanish without human presence; which everyday items may become immortalized as fossils; how copper pipes and wiring would be crushed into mere seams of reddish rock; why some of our earliest buildings might be the last architecture left; and how plastic, bronze sculpture, radio waves, and some man-made molecules may be our most lasting gifts to the universe. As he shows which human devastations are indelible, and which examples of our highest art and culture would endure longest, Weisman's narrative ultimately drives toward a radical but persuasive solution that needn't depend on our demise.--From publisher description.","pageCount":324,"publicationYear":2007,"displaySummary":"As he shows which human devastations are indelible, and which examples of our highest art and culture would endure longest, Weisman's narrative ultimately drives toward a radical but persuasive solution that needn't depend on our demise.--From publisher description.","primaryTopic":"Natural History & Animals","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Nature & Environment","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Nature & Environment","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Nature & Environment","mappedSubject":"Nature & Environment","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily explores the environmental impact of humanity and the potential consequences of our disappearance, positioning it firmly within Nature & Environment, while also touching on scientific and cultural aspects.","id":"subject-evidence-book-the-world-without-us-alan-weisman-1"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Nature","mappedSubject":"Nature & Environment","score":6,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-world-without-us-alan-weisman","id":"subject-evidence-book-the-world-without-us-alan-weisman-4"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Environmental degradation","mappedSubject":"Nature & Environment","score":6,"confidence"